# Abstract

**Draft.** Every number in this chapter is filled in by the generator from the result files named in
`data/frame-numbers.json`; nothing is typed by hand.

High-level synthesis (HLS) tools turn C/C++ into hardware, but the decisions that set the cost of the
result (which loops are pipelined, unrolled or flattened, how arrays are partitioned, which memories are
replicated, and how each memory is mapped onto the device's block RAM) are taken inside closed
commercial tools. A designer sees them only after synthesis, and existing estimators either model an
idealised HLS tool or learn aggregate numbers from many synthesis runs without explaining them.

This thesis takes a different route. It infers the decision procedure of one commercial tool, AMD Vitis
HLS {{vitis_versions}}, by black-box experiments: minimal kernels that vary one parameter at a time are
synthesised and the documented outputs of the tool (reports, transformation log, the LLVM IR it writes
between its stages) are read, together with AMD's open-sourced front end. The result is a catalogue of
{{n_notes}} numbered observations, each tied to its kernels and run directories; {{n_notes_confirmed}} of
them are marked confirmed, and the others are marked as only partly confirmed, as unconfirmed, or as
notes on measurement method. The rules are implemented as
a {{pipeline_passes}}-pass MLIR/CIRCT pipeline, *hls-est*, fed by a Polygeist (cgeist) front end that
compiles the real Vitis headers. hls-est predicts the block RAM (BRAM_18K) of every memory of a design and
explains each number.

hls-est is validated per design and per array against {{vitis_ok}} Vitis syntheses ({{vitis_parts}},
{{vitis_clocks}}, automatic throughput-driven partitioning off) of the {{designs}}-design HLSFactory
catalogue. Of the {{compared}} designs with both a Vitis result and an estimate, {{exact}}
({{exact_pct}} %) match Vitis exactly and {{in_goal}} ({{in_goal_pct}} %) are exact or over-count by at
most 15 %; the summed absolute error is {{abs_err}} BRAM_18K. The median estimate takes {{time_hlsest_median}} s
against {{time_vitis_median}} s for Vitis' C synthesis. The remaining errors are attributed per array to
open issues; the largest classes are loops hls-est does not schedule and loop-carried memory dependences
that do not yet reach its modulo scheduler. Every architectural assumption is exposed as one of
{{config_keys}} configuration keys or one of {{plugin_interfaces}} plugin interfaces. As an application,
hls-est's decisions are lowered to Verilog: {{rtl_verilog}} catalogue designs get RTL and
{{rtl_cosim_pass}} pass co-simulation against their own C testbenches, with no wrong-code mismatch.
